{:edb/db-before-t 2302 :edb/db-after-t 2303 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535615 50 #inst "2026-10-14T18:50:19.547Z" 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186133896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522923 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186133896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522925 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186133896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522926 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186133896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522927 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186133896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522929 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186133896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522930 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186133896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522934 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186133896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522939 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186133896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522941 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186133896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522954 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186133896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522969 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186133896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522980 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186133896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186522984 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186133896 1040 #edb/ref 17592186523014 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522988 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522923 1071 "![](images/19/19689860cee70dfdb45a3d2f6708bcdab42b82b3ea20ea352ee0b2060fe7be13.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨7\\sin(t),\\,7\\cos(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522923 1072 #uuid "0345c7e2-c321-58ac-aefe-5fccd5158803" 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522923 1074 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\nr'(t)=⟨7\\cos(t),\\,-7\\sin(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}7\\cos(t)\\cdot 7\\cos(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}49\\cos^{2}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522924 1080 "144 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}" 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522924 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522924 1083 #uuid "04f22653-76d9-5256-9778-31f07dcd3d94" 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186522933 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522925 1071 "![](images/e0/e0ac24d590f74a62bd45885b90190b82018372fd086d8cdda6ee8fde115d6fdd.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨6{e}^{\\sin(t)},\\,2{e}^{\\cos(t)}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522925 1072 #uuid "04fa4661-86e7-5801-8b1a-25ba13abd1bf" 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522925 1074 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨6\\cos(t)\\,{e}^{\\sin(t)},\\,-2\\sin(t)\\,{e}^{\\cos(t)}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2{e}^{\\cos(t)}\\cdot 6\\cos(t)\\,{e}^{\\sin(t)}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }12\\cos(t)\\,{e}^{\\cos(t)+\\sin(t)}\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523000 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522926 1071 "![](images/3f/3fc4346f3603d6d844584973c30b8ecf1e738a4d0a393ed1ab3ec62f764596cc.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨12\\sin(t),\\,12\\cos(t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522926 1072 #uuid "0660c121-2d4f-5acb-9dc8-3ef2b434821c" 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522926 1074 "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\mathrm{d}A\\\\&=-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-\\oint_C(P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y)=\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\nr'(t)=⟨12\\cos(t),\\,-12\\sin(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_Cy\\,\\mathrm{d}x&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}12\\cos(t)\\cdot 12\\cos(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}144\\cos^{2}(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522927 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523008 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522927 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535615 17592186045443 true] [17592186522927 1071 "![](images/70/70ef514109413878d6cafb2931b51ee1d1b48eca053d4c085f0ebe5e8170aa3e.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\sin(t),\\,6\\sin(2t)⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhe